1
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:14,520
And they came to hollow Lacedaemon, land of ravines,
and drove to the house of glorious Menelaus.

2
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:17,260
They found him giving a wedding-feast to his many kinsmen

3
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:20,900
for his son and his blameless daughter, in his own house.

4
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:24,900
The daughter he was sending to the
son of Achilles, breaker of ranks:

5
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:28,540
in Troy long ago he had promised her, had bowed his head

6
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:33,000
to give her, and now the gods were
bringing the marriage to pass.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
So now with horses and chariots he was sending her forth

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,960
to the famous city of the Myrmidons, whom that man ruled.

9
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:43,680
For his son he was bringing home
Alector's daughter out of Sparta —

10
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:47,000
the son born to him in his strength,
well-loved Megapenthes,

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,740
out of a slave-woman. For to Helen
the gods showed no more children

12
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:57,360
after the first time, when she bore her lovely daughter
Hermione, who had the beauty of golden Aphrodite.

13
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:02,820
So they were feasting through the great high-roofed house,
the neighbors and kinsmen of glorious Menelaus,

14
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:08,800
taking their pleasure; and among them a godlike
singer sang to the lyre, and two tumblers among them,

15
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,820
as he led the song, went spinning through their midst.

16
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:15,160
And the two of them halted at the
gateway of the house, themselves

17
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:20,120
and their horses — the hero
Telemachus and Nestor's shining son.

18
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,220
Lord Eteoneus came out and saw them,
the busy henchman of glorious Menelaus,

19
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:28,700
and went through the house to bring
the news to the shepherd of the people;

20
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,820
standing close he spoke winged words to him:

21
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:38,020
"Two strangers here, Menelaus, nurtured of Zeus —
two men, and they look like the stock of great Zeus.

22
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:43,760
Tell me: do we unyoke their swift horses for them,
or send them on to another man, who may welcome them?"

23
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:47,060
Then fair-haired Menelaus, greatly stirred, replied:

24
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:52,980
"You were no fool before, Eteoneus, son of Boethous —
but now you are babbling nonsense like a child.

25
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:58,300
The two of us ate plenty of strangers' bread,
you and I, on the road home, hoping Zeus might someday

26
00:01:58,300 --> 00:02:02,140
give us rest from misery. Unyoke the strangers' horses,

27
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:05,280
and bring the men themselves in, to share the feast."

28
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:09,300
So he spoke, and the man dashed back
through the hall, and called the other

29
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:11,640
busy henchmen to come along with him.

30
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:16,740
They loosed the sweating horses from under the
yoke and tied them up at the mangers of the horses,

31
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:19,840
and threw them spelt, and mixed white barley in with it,

32
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:23,020
and leaned the chariot against the gleaming entrance-wall,

33
00:02:23,020 --> 00:02:25,840
and led the men into the godlike house. And they stared

34
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:28,720
in wonder through the house of the Zeus-nurtured king:

35
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:34,260
for there was a sheen as of the sun or the moon
through the high-roofed house of glorious Menelaus.

36
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:39,280
And when they had had their joy of gazing,
they stepped into the polished baths and bathed.

37
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,480
And when the slave-women had bathed
them and rubbed them with oil,

38
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:49,540
and thrown about them fleecy cloaks and tunics,
they sat down on high seats beside Menelaus, Atreus' son.

39
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:52,200
A handmaid brought water for their hands in a pitcher,

40
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,220
a beautiful golden one, and poured it over a silver basin

41
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:59,200
for them to wash, and drew up
a polished table beside them.

42
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:02,840
The grave housekeeper brought
bread and set it before them,

43
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:09,280
laying out delicacies in plenty, giving gladly of her
store; a carver lifted platters of meat of every kind

44
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:12,800
and set them down, and placed gold cups beside them.

45
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,960
And fair-haired Menelaus greeted
them with a wave of the hand:

46
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:21,220
"Take of the bread, and be welcome. And afterward,
when you have tasted supper, we will ask what men

47
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:27,200
you are: for the stock of your parents is not lost in you —
you are of the stock of scepter-bearing kings,

48
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:30,900
nurtured of Zeus. No base men could father such sons."

49
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:35,100
So he spoke, and set before them
the fat roast chine of an ox,

50
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:38,860
lifting it in his hands —
the portion of honor they had set for him.

51
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:42,120
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

52
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:47,460
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,
then Telemachus spoke to the son of Nestor,

53
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:50,680
leaning his head close, so that the others might not hear:

54
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:56,040
"Look, son of Nestor, delight of my heart —
the lightning of bronze through these echoing halls,

55
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:59,780
the gold and the amber, the silver and the ivory!

56
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:02,800
Such, I think, is the court of Olympian Zeus within:

57
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,880
all this here, past speaking!
Awe takes me, looking at it."

58
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:12,780
But fair-haired Menelaus caught what he was saying,
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to them:

59
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:16,280
"Dear children, with Zeus no mortal man could vie:

60
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:19,060
his halls and his possessions are deathless things.

61
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:22,640
Among men, another may match me in wealth — or may not.

62
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:28,800
Much I suffered for it, and far I wandered to bring
it home in my ships: eight years gone before I came,

63
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:32,360
driven wandering to Cyprus and
Phoenicia and the Egyptians;

64
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:38,880
I reached the Ethiopians, the Sidonians, the Erembi,
and Libya, where the lambs come horned from the birth,

65
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,040
for the ewes bear three times
in the full round of the year.

66
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:46,400
There no lord and no shepherd goes wanting
for cheese and meat and sweet milk:

67
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:50,360
the flocks give milk for the milking all the year round.

68
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:55,780
But while I roamed those countries gathering
much substance, another man killed my brother —

69
00:04:55,780 --> 00:04:59,860
by stealth, off guard, through
the treachery of his cursed wife.

70
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:02,800
So I take no joy in being lord of all this wealth.

71
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:06,680
You will have heard it from
your fathers, whoever they are:

72
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:11,240
I have suffered much, and I lost a house
well lived-in, full of many splendid things.

73
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:16,320
Would I lived here with a third part only of all this,
and the men were alive who died in those years

74
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:20,160
in wide Troy, far from horse-grazing Argos!

75
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:24,460
And yet — though I mourn them all,
and grieve, often, sitting here in our halls,

76
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:30,620
and one hour ease my heart with weeping, and the next
leave off, for men are quickly sated of cold lament —

77
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:33,380
for all of them together I do not mourn so much,

78
00:05:33,380 --> 00:05:37,780
grieved though I am, as for one man,
who makes sleep hateful to me,

79
00:05:37,780 --> 00:05:44,860
and food, when I remember: for no Achaean labored as
Odysseus labored and endured. And the end of it was

80
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:50,700
sorrow for him, and for me a grief never to be forgotten —
that he is gone so long, and we know nothing,

81
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:57,360
whether he lives or is dead. They must be mourning
him now, old Laertes, and steady-hearted Penelope,

82
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:00,680
and Telemachus, whom he left new-born in the house."

83
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:04,860
So he spoke, and stirred in the boy
the ache to weep for his father:

84
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:08,040
tears fell from his eyelids to
the ground at his father's name,

85
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:13,740
and he lifted the purple cloak before his eyes
with both hands. And Menelaus noticed him,

86
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:18,920
and pondered then in his mind and in his heart
whether to leave it to him to name his father,

87
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:22,000
or to question him first and test him point by point.

88
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,800
While he was turning this over
in his mind and in his heart,

89
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:32,140
out of her fragrant high-roofed chamber came Helen,
looking like Artemis of the golden distaff;

90
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:38,560
and with her Adreste set out her well-made
reclining chair, Alcippe brought a rug of soft wool,

91
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:44,900
and Phylo brought her silver basket, given her
by Alcandre, wife of Polybus, who lived in Thebes

92
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,840
of Egypt, where the greatest wealth lies stored in houses:

93
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:54,200
he gave Menelaus two silver baths and a
pair of tripods and ten talents of gold;

94
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:58,240
and apart from these his wife gave
Helen beautiful gifts of her own —

95
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:05,020
a golden distaff she gave, and a basket on wheels,
silver, its rims finished off in gold.

96
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:10,660
This the handmaid Phylo brought and set beside her,
stuffed with fine-spun yarn, and across it

97
00:07:10,660 --> 00:07:14,840
lay the distaff, wound with wool of violet darkness.

98
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:18,260
She sat down on the chair, with a footstool for her feet,

99
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:22,040
and at once began asking her husband about everything:

100
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:25,400
"Do we know yet, Menelaus, nurtured of Zeus, who these men

101
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:28,660
declare themselves to be, who have come to our house?

102
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:32,680
Shall I lie, or speak the truth? My heart bids me speak.

103
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:39,180
For never yet, I say, have I seen such a likeness,
in man or woman — awe takes me, looking at him —

104
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:42,360
as this boy is like the son of great-hearted Odysseus:

105
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:45,800
Telemachus, whom that man left new-born in the house

106
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:52,680
when for my sake — bitch-faced that I was —
you Achaeans came up under Troy, with bold war in your hearts."

107
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,540
Then fair-haired Menelaus answered her:

108
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:59,000
"I too see it now, wife, just as you liken him:

109
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,020
those were the very feet of the man, the very hands,
the glance of his eyes, the head, the hair above.

110
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:08,840
And just now, remembering Odysseus,
I was telling of all he labored and endured

111
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:13,260
for my sake — and the boy shed a
bitter tear from under his brows,

112
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:15,920
holding the purple cloak up before his eyes."

113
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:19,560
Then Peisistratus, Nestor's son, answered him:

114
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:26,440
"Menelaus, son of Atreus, Zeus-nurtured, leader of
peoples, this is indeed that man's son, as you say.

115
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:30,860
But he is modest, and his heart is shy of
splashing forth big words on a first arrival

116
00:08:30,860 --> 00:08:35,100
in front of you, whose voice
delights us both like a god's.

117
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:40,020
Gerenian Nestor the horseman sent me out to
come with him as escort: he longed to see you,

118
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:42,720
in hope you might set some word or work before him.

119
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:48,540
For a son has many sorrows in the house when his
father is gone and no other helpers stand by him —

120
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:54,540
as now with Telemachus: the father gone,
and no others in all the land to beat trouble away."

121
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,940
Then fair-haired Menelaus answered him:

122
00:08:56,940 --> 00:09:03,920
"Well now! Truly the son of a beloved man is in my house,
a man who fought through many trials for my sake;

123
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:06,960
and I used to say that when he
came I would welcome him beyond all

124
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:10,100
the other Argives — if Olympian Zeus of the wide voice

125
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:14,280
gave the two of us a homecoming
over the sea in our fast ships.

126
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:17,780
I would have settled him a city
in Argos, and built him a house,

127
00:09:17,780 --> 00:09:23,460
and brought him out of Ithaca with his goods and
his son and all his people — emptying out one town

128
00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:26,860
of those that lie around and own me as their lord.

129
00:09:26,860 --> 00:09:29,600
Being here, we would have mingled often; and nothing

130
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:33,360
would have parted us then,
loving and delighting in each other,

131
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:36,140
until death's black cloud came folding round us.

132
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:40,400
But such things, I suppose,
the god himself must have grudged,

133
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:45,420
who made that unlucky man — him alone —
the man who never came home."

134
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:49,020
So he spoke, and stirred in them all the ache of lament.

135
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:55,260
Argive Helen wept, the daughter of Zeus,
and Telemachus wept, and Menelaus, Atreus' son,

136
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:57,820
nor could the son of Nestor keep his eyes dry:

137
00:09:57,820 --> 00:10:03,540
for his heart remembered blameless Antilochus,
whom the shining son of bright Dawn had killed.

138
00:10:03,540 --> 00:10:06,120
Remembering him, he spoke winged words:

139
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:08,480
"Son of Atreus, old Nestor always said

140
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:11,620
you were clear-headed beyond other
mortals, whenever we remembered you

141
00:10:11,620 --> 00:10:14,620
in his halls, and questioned one another.

142
00:10:14,620 --> 00:10:20,900
So now, if it may be, listen to me. I take no
pleasure in weeping after supper — and dawn

143
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:27,940
will come, early-born, soon enough. Not that I grudge
lament for any mortal who dies and meets his doom:

144
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:33,980
this is the only honor left to miserable mortals,
to cut a lock of hair and let a tear fall down the cheek.

145
00:10:33,980 --> 00:10:39,640
For my brother died too — hardly the meanest of
the Argives. You would know him. I myself never

146
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:43,940
met him, never saw him. But beyond other men, they say,

147
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:47,400
Antilochus was surpassing swift of foot, and a fighter."

148
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:49,680
Then fair-haired Menelaus answered him:

149
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:54,920
"Dear friend — you have said all a clear-headed
man could say or do, even a man older than you.

150
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:59,140
From such a father you come;
hence your clear-headed speech.

151
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:02,240
Easily known is the seed of a
man for whom the son of Cronos

152
00:11:02,240 --> 00:11:08,380
spins out happiness at his marriage and his begetting —
as now he has granted Nestor, through all his days,

153
00:11:08,380 --> 00:11:13,840
to grow old sleek in his own halls,
and his sons to be wise and the best of spearmen.

154
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:19,480
So we will let the weeping go, which came on us before,
and think again of supper. Let them pour water

155
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:25,420
over our hands. And at dawn there will be tales again
for Telemachus and me to tell through to each other."

156
00:11:25,420 --> 00:11:31,420
So he spoke, and Asphalion poured water over their
hands, the busy henchman of glorious Menelaus,

157
00:11:31,420 --> 00:11:35,260
and their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

158
00:11:35,260 --> 00:11:39,220
Then Helen, daughter of Zeus, thought of one thing more:

159
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:42,020
into the wine they were drinking
she straightway cast a drug

160
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:46,700
that undoes grief and anger and
brings forgetfulness of every evil.

161
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:52,760
Whoever swallowed it down, once mixed in the bowl,
would let no tear fall down his cheeks that day,

162
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:55,660
not though his mother and his father lay there dead,

163
00:11:55,660 --> 00:12:01,320
not though before his face men put his brother or
his own son to the bronze, and his own eyes saw it.

164
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:08,200
Such were the subtle drugs the daughter of Zeus possessed,
good drugs, given her by Polydamna, wife of Thon,

165
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:11,300
the Egyptian — for there the grain-giving plowland bears

166
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:17,840
drugs in greatest plenty, many good in the mixing,
many baneful; and every man there is a healer beyond all

167
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:21,240
humankind: for they are of the race of Paeeon.

168
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:27,180
When she had cast it in, and bidden the wine be
poured, she took up the talk again, and said:

169
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:33,700
"Menelaus, son of Atreus, nurtured of Zeus — and you,
sons of good men. To this man and that Zeus gives

170
00:12:33,700 --> 00:12:37,900
good and evil, now one, now another:
for he can do all things.

171
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:40,320
So now — feast where you sit in the halls

172
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:44,560
and take your pleasure in tales.
And I will tell one that fits the hour.

173
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:50,100
All of it I could not tell, could not so much as number —
all the trials of enduring-hearted Odysseus;

174
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:52,340
but here is one thing that mighty man did and dared

175
00:12:52,340 --> 00:12:56,540
in the Trojans' country,
where you Achaeans suffered your pains.

176
00:12:56,540 --> 00:13:02,360
He beat his own body with disfiguring blows,
threw a foul rag over his shoulders, and like a house-slave

177
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:07,620
he slipped into the wide-wayed city of his enemies —
hiding himself, in the likeness of another man,

178
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:11,840
a beggar: he who was nothing of
the kind by the Achaean ships.

179
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:14,920
In that shape he slipped into the
Trojans' city, and all of them

180
00:13:14,920 --> 00:13:17,820
were taken in. I alone knew him for what he was,

181
00:13:17,820 --> 00:13:21,600
and questioned him; and he in
his cunning kept slipping away.

182
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:24,740
But when at last I was bathing
him and rubbing him with oil,

183
00:13:24,740 --> 00:13:29,380
and had put clothes on him, and sworn a strong
oath not to reveal Odysseus to the Trojans

184
00:13:29,380 --> 00:13:35,760
before he reached the fast ships and the huts,
then at last he told me all the Achaeans had in mind.

185
00:13:35,760 --> 00:13:38,200
And he killed many Trojans with the long-edged bronze

186
00:13:38,200 --> 00:13:42,220
on his way back to the Argives,
and brought back a harvest of knowledge.

187
00:13:42,220 --> 00:13:45,180
Then the other women of Troy wailed shrill. But my heart

188
00:13:45,180 --> 00:13:48,120
rejoiced — for my heart had turned already toward going

189
00:13:48,120 --> 00:13:51,160
home again, and I mourned too late the madness Aphrodite

190
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:54,900
gave me, when she led me there from my own dear country,

191
00:13:54,900 --> 00:14:00,560
forsaking my child, my bridal chamber,
and a husband lacking nothing, in mind or in beauty."

192
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:02,760
Then fair-haired Menelaus answered her:

193
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:06,420
"Yes, wife: all this you have
told rightly, in due measure.

194
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:11,280
By now I have learned the counsel and the mind of
many heroes, and traveled over much of the earth,

195
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:15,820
but never yet have my eyes looked on such a
one as the heart of enduring-hearted Odysseus.

196
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:21,540
Here is one more thing that mighty man did and dared
inside the carved horse, where we sat, all the best

197
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:25,100
of the Argives, carrying death and doom to the Trojans.

198
00:14:25,100 --> 00:14:30,720
Then you came there — some spirit must have moved you,
one that willed to hand glory to the Trojans —

199
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:33,560
and godlike Deiphobus came following after you.

200
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:39,540
Three times you circled the hollow ambush, stroking it,
and you called by name the best of the Danaans,

201
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:42,320
making your voice the voice of each Argive's wife.

202
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:48,500
And I and the son of Tydeus and noble Odysseus,
sitting in the middle of them, heard you calling;

203
00:14:48,500 --> 00:14:53,160
and the two of us were wild to spring up
and come out, or answer at once from within.

204
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:56,980
But Odysseus held us down,
and checked us for all our longing.

205
00:14:56,980 --> 00:15:02,660
Then all the other sons of the Achaeans kept silence;
Anticlus alone was bent on giving you an answer —

206
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:05,160
but Odysseus clamped his great hands over his mouth

207
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:09,500
and held them there, unyielding,
and saved all the Achaeans:

208
00:15:09,500 --> 00:15:14,180
he held him so until Pallas Athena led you away."

209
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:17,360
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

210
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:21,960
"Menelaus, son of Atreus,
Zeus-nurtured, leader of peoples —

211
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:28,540
the more grief: for none of this kept bitter death from him,
not though the heart inside him had been iron.

212
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:35,300
But come — send us to bed now, so that at last
we may lie down and take our joy of sweet sleep."

213
00:15:35,300 --> 00:15:38,040
So he spoke; and Argive Helen ordered the slave-women

214
00:15:38,040 --> 00:15:44,980
to set out bedsteads under the porch, and to lay on
them beautiful purple blankets, and spread rugs above,

215
00:15:44,980 --> 00:15:48,240
and fleecy cloaks on top of all, for cover.

216
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:54,520
Out of the hall they went with torches in their hands
and made the beds; and a herald led the guests out.

217
00:15:54,520 --> 00:16:00,140
So they slept there in the forecourt of the house,
the hero Telemachus and Nestor's shining son;

218
00:16:00,140 --> 00:16:03,580
but Atreus' son slept in the inner room of the high house,

219
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:07,560
and beside him lay long-robed Helen, shining among women.

220
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:11,440
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

221
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:14,380
Menelaus of the great war-cry rose up from his bed,

222
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:18,600
put on his clothes, slung his
sharp sword about his shoulder,

223
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:24,720
bound the beautiful sandals beneath his shining feet,
and came out of his bedroom looking like a god.

224
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:29,460
He sat down beside Telemachus,
and spoke a word, and named him:

225
00:16:29,460 --> 00:16:35,580
"What need has brought you here, hero Telemachus,
to shining Lacedaemon, over the broad back of the sea?

226
00:16:35,580 --> 00:16:39,840
A public matter or private?
Tell me the plain truth of it."

227
00:16:39,840 --> 00:16:42,580
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

228
00:16:42,580 --> 00:16:46,720
"Menelaus, son of Atreus,
Zeus-nurtured, leader of peoples,

229
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:50,040
I came in hope you might tell me some word of my father.

230
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:56,660
My house is being eaten; my rich fields are ruined;
the house is packed with enemies, who day upon day

231
00:16:56,660 --> 00:17:00,580
slaughter my thronging flocks and
my shambling crook-horned cattle —

232
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:02,960
my mother's suitors, with their overweening insolence.

233
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:09,380
That is why I come now to your knees: in hope you will
consent to tell his bitter death — whether you saw it

234
00:17:09,380 --> 00:17:12,880
with your own eyes, or heard the tale of him from another

235
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:15,740
wanderer. Beyond all men his mother bore him for sorrow.

236
00:17:15,740 --> 00:17:18,580
Do not soften anything from courtesy or pity:

237
00:17:18,580 --> 00:17:21,260
tell me fully whatever sight you came upon.

238
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:26,500
I beg you — if ever my father, good Odysseus,
promised you word or work and made it good

239
00:17:26,500 --> 00:17:29,660
in the Trojans' country,
where you Achaeans suffered your pains,

240
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:33,600
remember it for me now: tell me the unerring truth."

241
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:36,840
Then fair-haired Menelaus, greatly stirred, replied:

242
00:17:36,840 --> 00:17:39,960
"Shame on them! Truly in the bed of a strong-hearted man

243
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:43,580
they want to lie — the spineless creatures that they are!

244
00:17:43,580 --> 00:17:48,340
As when a deer beds down her newborn suckling
fawns in the thicket-lair of a mighty lion,

245
00:17:48,340 --> 00:17:54,220
and goes questing the spurs and grassy hollows,
grazing — and then the lion comes back to his own bed

246
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:56,900
and lets an ugly doom loose on them both:

247
00:17:56,900 --> 00:18:00,140
so Odysseus will let an ugly doom loose on those men.

248
00:18:00,140 --> 00:18:06,560
If only — father Zeus, Athena, Apollo! —
the man he was long ago in strong-founded Lesbos,

249
00:18:06,560 --> 00:18:12,720
when he rose on a challenge and wrestled Philomeleides
and threw him hard, and all the Achaeans cheered:

250
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:16,580
such as he was then, let that
Odysseus close with the suitors:

251
00:18:16,580 --> 00:18:19,780
quick would their fates be then,
and bitter their weddings.

252
00:18:19,780 --> 00:18:26,080
As for what you ask of me and beg me: I will not talk
around it, swerving aside, and I will not deceive you.

253
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:31,140
What the unerring Old Man of the Sea told me,
not one word of it will I hide or cover from you.

254
00:18:31,140 --> 00:18:34,900
In Egypt the gods still held me, hungry as I was for home,

255
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:37,440
because I had not offered them their full hecatombs —

256
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:40,600
and the gods will always have
their commandments remembered.

257
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:45,780
Now there is an island in the much-surging sea
off the mouth of Egypt — Pharos, men call it,

258
00:18:45,780 --> 00:18:49,960
as far out as a hollow ship runs in a whole
day with a whistling wind astern of her —

259
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:53,120
and in it a harbor, good anchorage, from which men launch

260
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:56,800
their trim ships to sea, once they have drawn black water.

261
00:18:56,800 --> 00:19:02,220
There the gods held me twenty days: never once
the sea-winds blowing fair, that speed ships

262
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:04,220
over the broad back of the sea.

263
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:07,500
And now all our stores would have
been spent, and the men's strength,

264
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:11,000
had not one of the gods pitied me and saved me:

265
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:17,720
the daughter of mighty Proteus, the Old Man of the Sea —
Eidothea. For I stirred her heart beyond the rest

266
00:19:17,720 --> 00:19:21,280
when she met me wandering alone,
apart from my companions —

267
00:19:21,280 --> 00:19:27,060
for they were forever roving round the island,
fishing with bent hooks, their bellies pinched with hunger.

268
00:19:27,060 --> 00:19:29,540
She stood close by me and spoke, and said:

269
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:33,240
'Are you a fool, stranger — so slack, so loose of wit?

270
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:36,600
Or do you slacken by choice, and enjoy your suffering?

271
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:42,400
So long you are held on the island, and can find no
ending of it, while your companions' hearts grow small.'

272
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:44,060
So she spoke, and I answered her:

273
00:19:44,060 --> 00:19:48,060
'I will say this straight,
whichever of the goddesses you are:

274
00:19:48,060 --> 00:19:54,640
I am not held here by any will of mine. I must have
sinned against the deathless ones who hold wide heaven.

275
00:19:54,640 --> 00:20:00,700
But tell me — for the gods know all things — which of
the immortals shackles me here and has bound my road,

276
00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:04,320
and my homecoming — how I shall
go over the fish-filled sea.'

277
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:08,220
So I spoke, and the shining goddess answered at once:

278
00:20:08,220 --> 00:20:10,960
'Then I will tell you, stranger, and tell you truly.

279
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:16,780
There haunts this place an unerring Old Man of the Sea,
deathless Proteus of Egypt, who knows the depths

280
00:20:16,780 --> 00:20:19,940
of every sea — Poseidon's under-servant.

281
00:20:19,940 --> 00:20:22,860
He, they say, is my father, who begot me.

282
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:25,740
If you could somehow set an ambush and take hold of him,

283
00:20:25,740 --> 00:20:28,540
he would tell you the road,
and the measures of your journey,

284
00:20:28,540 --> 00:20:32,100
and your homecoming — how you
shall go over the fish-filled sea.

285
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:36,880
And he would tell you too, Zeus-nurtured, if you wish,
whatever evil and good have been done in your halls

286
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:40,240
while you have been gone on your long and grinding road.'

287
00:20:40,240 --> 00:20:42,620
So she spoke, and I answered her:

288
00:20:42,620 --> 00:20:49,220
'Devise the ambush yourself, for this old god —
or he will see me first, or know beforehand, and escape me.

289
00:20:49,220 --> 00:20:51,760
A god is hard for a mortal man to master.'

290
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:54,700
So I spoke, and the shining goddess answered at once:

291
00:20:54,700 --> 00:20:57,620
'Then I will tell you, stranger, and tell you truly.

292
00:20:57,620 --> 00:21:03,420
When the sun bestrides the middle of heaven,
then the unerring Old Man of the Sea comes out of the water

293
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:06,840
under the West Wind's breath,
hidden in the dark shiver of the sea,

294
00:21:06,840 --> 00:21:09,600
and coming out he beds down under the hollow caves;

295
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:12,680
and around him seals, the brood
of the sea's lovely daughter,

296
00:21:12,680 --> 00:21:18,460
sleep in a crowd, come up out of the gray salt water,
breathing out the sharp smell of the deep brine.

297
00:21:18,460 --> 00:21:24,140
There I will bring you at the showing of dawn and bed
you down in the row. Choose well three companions,

298
00:21:24,140 --> 00:21:26,280
the best you have by the well-benched ships.

299
00:21:26,280 --> 00:21:28,340
And I will tell you all the old man's tricks.

300
00:21:28,340 --> 00:21:34,160
First he will count his seals, and go over them;
and when he has told them off by fives and seen them all,

301
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:37,300
he will lie down in their midst,
like a herdsman in his flocks.

302
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:41,520
The moment you see him settled down,
then let strength and force be all your thought:

303
00:21:41,520 --> 00:21:45,380
hold him there for all his struggling,
however he strains to escape.

304
00:21:45,380 --> 00:21:48,160
He will make trial of everything, turning into all things

305
00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:52,080
that creep upon the earth —
into water, into god-kindled fire;

306
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:54,720
but hold him unshakably, and press him the harder.

307
00:21:54,720 --> 00:21:59,840
And when at last he questions you with words himself,
in the same shape you saw him settle to sleep,

308
00:21:59,840 --> 00:22:05,640
then leave off your force, hero: set the old man loose,
and ask him which of the gods bears hard on you,

309
00:22:05,640 --> 00:22:09,220
and your homecoming — how you
shall go over the fish-filled sea.'

310
00:22:09,220 --> 00:22:12,000
So speaking she plunged under the surging sea.

311
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:17,320
And I went to the ships where they stood in the sand,
and my heart ran dark with many things as I went.

312
00:22:17,320 --> 00:22:22,800
And when I had come down to the ship and the sea,
we made our supper, and ambrosial night came on,

313
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:25,660
and we lay down to sleep on the surf-line of the sea.

314
00:22:25,660 --> 00:22:31,960
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,
I set out along the shore of the wide-wayed sea,

315
00:22:31,960 --> 00:22:37,860
praying hard to the gods; and I took three companions,
the three I trusted most for every venture.

316
00:22:37,860 --> 00:22:43,140
She meanwhile had slipped under the wide gulf of the
sea and brought up out of the deep four sealskins,

317
00:22:43,140 --> 00:22:46,700
all fresh-flayed: she was plotting
an ambush against her own father.

318
00:22:46,700 --> 00:22:52,220
She had scooped out beds in the sea-sand and sat
there waiting. And we came up close beside her,

319
00:22:52,220 --> 00:22:55,780
and she bedded us down in the row,
and threw a skin over each.

320
00:22:55,780 --> 00:22:58,660
There our ambush would have been most horrible: the deadly

321
00:22:58,660 --> 00:23:01,740
stench of the salt-bred seals ground us down horribly —

322
00:23:01,740 --> 00:23:04,480
who would lay himself down beside a beast of the sea? —

323
00:23:04,480 --> 00:23:07,980
but she herself saved us; she devised a great relief:

324
00:23:07,980 --> 00:23:11,260
she brought ambrosia and set it under each man's nose,

325
00:23:11,260 --> 00:23:14,220
breathing sweetness,
and it killed the stench of the beast.

326
00:23:14,220 --> 00:23:20,840
All the morning we waited, with enduring hearts,
and the seals came crowding up out of the sea, and then

327
00:23:20,840 --> 00:23:24,040
lay down in rows along the surf-line of the sea;

328
00:23:24,040 --> 00:23:27,800
and at midday the old man came
out of the sea, and found his fat

329
00:23:27,800 --> 00:23:31,280
seals, and went over them all, and counted the number —

330
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:37,460
and among the beasts he counted us the first,
his heart never guessing the trick. Then he too lay down.

331
00:23:37,460 --> 00:23:39,860
Then we rushed him with a shout, and threw our arms

332
00:23:39,860 --> 00:23:45,820
around him; and the old man did not forget his cunning art,
but first he turned to a great-bearded lion,

333
00:23:45,820 --> 00:23:52,380
and then to a serpent, a leopard, a huge boar;
he turned to running water, to a tree in towering leaf;

334
00:23:52,380 --> 00:23:55,540
and we held on unshakably, with enduring hearts.

335
00:23:55,540 --> 00:24:01,680
But when at last the old man wearied, for all his tricks,
then he questioned me with words, and said:

336
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:04,200
'Which of the gods, son of Atreus, laid the plan with you

337
00:24:04,200 --> 00:24:08,040
to take me by ambush against my will? What do you need?'

338
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:09,940
So he spoke, and I answered him:

339
00:24:09,940 --> 00:24:15,620
'You know, old man — why ask me sideways? —
how long I am held on this island, and can find

340
00:24:15,620 --> 00:24:18,860
no ending of it, and the heart inside me grows small.

341
00:24:18,860 --> 00:24:25,040
But tell me — for the gods know all things — which of
the immortals shackles me here and has bound my road,

342
00:24:25,040 --> 00:24:29,080
and my homecoming — how I shall
go over the fish-filled sea.'

343
00:24:29,080 --> 00:24:31,420
So I spoke, and he answered me at once:

344
00:24:31,420 --> 00:24:37,100
'But you should have offered fair victims to Zeus and
the other gods before embarking, to come most quickly

345
00:24:37,100 --> 00:24:40,440
to your own country, sailing the wine-dark sea.

346
00:24:40,440 --> 00:24:46,380
For it is not your portion to see your own people,
to reach your well-built house and your own fatherland,

347
00:24:46,380 --> 00:24:52,020
before you go back again to the waters of Egypt,
the river fallen from Zeus, and offer holy hecatombs

348
00:24:52,020 --> 00:24:55,040
to the deathless gods who hold wide heaven.

349
00:24:55,040 --> 00:24:57,860
Then the gods will give you the road you long for.'

350
00:24:57,860 --> 00:25:02,600
So he spoke, and my heart broke within me,
because he sent me back over the misted sea

351
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:05,880
to Egypt again — the long and grinding road.

352
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:07,860
Even so I answered him and said:

353
00:25:07,860 --> 00:25:11,340
'All this I will do, old man, just as you command.

354
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:14,280
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

355
00:25:14,280 --> 00:25:17,240
did all the Achaeans come home unharmed in their ships,

356
00:25:17,240 --> 00:25:20,580
all whom Nestor and I left
behind when we sailed from Troy?

357
00:25:20,580 --> 00:25:23,340
Or did any die a bitter death aboard his ship,

358
00:25:23,340 --> 00:25:27,300
or in the arms of his friends,
once he had wound the war to its end?'

359
00:25:27,300 --> 00:25:29,680
So I spoke, and he answered me at once:

360
00:25:29,680 --> 00:25:35,740
'Son of Atreus, why ask me this? You have no need to know,
no need to learn my mind. Not long, I tell you,

361
00:25:35,740 --> 00:25:38,860
will you keep from tears,
once you have heard the whole of it.

362
00:25:38,860 --> 00:25:43,860
Many of them were broken, and many were left;
but of the leaders of the bronze-shirted Achaeans, two

363
00:25:43,860 --> 00:25:47,380
alone died on the homeward way —
the fighting you saw yourself —

364
00:25:47,380 --> 00:25:51,060
and one, still living somewhere,
is held back on the wide sea.

365
00:25:51,060 --> 00:25:54,060
Ajax was broken among his long-oared ships.

366
00:25:54,060 --> 00:25:58,780
Poseidon drove him first against the great rocks
of Gyrae — and yet he saved him from the sea;

367
00:25:58,780 --> 00:26:02,400
and he would have escaped his doom,
though Athena hated him,

368
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:06,300
had he not flung out an overweening
word in his great blindness:

369
00:26:06,300 --> 00:26:10,860
he said it was in the gods' despite he
had escaped the great gulf of the sea.

370
00:26:10,860 --> 00:26:15,560
Poseidon heard his loud boasting,
and straightway took his trident in his massive hands

371
00:26:15,560 --> 00:26:19,300
and struck the rock of Gyrae, and split it apart:

372
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:22,960
one part held its place,
but the broken piece fell in the sea —

373
00:26:22,960 --> 00:26:26,280
the piece where Ajax sat when
the great blindness took him —

374
00:26:26,280 --> 00:26:29,720
and it carried him down into the boundless surging deep.

375
00:26:29,720 --> 00:26:32,820
So he died there, when he had drunk the salt water.

376
00:26:32,820 --> 00:26:39,080
But your brother somehow slipped the fates,
escaped them in his hollow ships: the lady Hera saved him.

377
00:26:39,080 --> 00:26:44,060
Yet when he was all but come to the steep height
of Malea, then a storm-wind snatched him up

378
00:26:44,060 --> 00:26:50,420
and carried him, groaning heavily, over the fish-filled
sea to the border of the country where Thyestes once

379
00:26:50,420 --> 00:26:55,000
had his house, and now Thyestes' son Aegisthus lived.

380
00:26:55,000 --> 00:27:01,940
But when from there too a safe homecoming showed,
and the gods turned the wind fair, and they reached home —

381
00:27:01,940 --> 00:27:07,940
oh, rejoicing he set foot on his fathers' land,
and touched his country and kissed it: the hot tears

382
00:27:07,940 --> 00:27:11,680
streamed from him, so glad he was to see his land.

383
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:14,800
But from a lookout a watchman saw him — posted there

384
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:18,100
by Aegisthus of the crooked counsels, who had promised him

385
00:27:18,100 --> 00:27:22,160
two talents of gold for wages: a full year he had watched,

386
00:27:22,160 --> 00:27:27,100
so the king might not slip past unseen
and remember his fighting strength.

387
00:27:27,100 --> 00:27:30,660
The man went with his news to the house,
to the shepherd of the people,

388
00:27:30,660 --> 00:27:33,760
and at once Aegisthus laid his treacherous craft:

389
00:27:33,760 --> 00:27:35,760
he picked the twenty best men in the land

390
00:27:35,760 --> 00:27:40,500
and set an ambush, and across the
hall he ordered a feast made ready;

391
00:27:40,500 --> 00:27:43,900
and then he went to summon Agamemnon,
shepherd of the people,

392
00:27:43,900 --> 00:27:48,160
with horses and chariots —
and unspeakable things in his mind.

393
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:54,520
He led him up unwitting of his death, and killed him
at the dinner — as a man cuts down an ox at the manger.

394
00:27:54,520 --> 00:27:58,360
Not one of the companions of Atreus'
son was left, who followed him,

395
00:27:58,360 --> 00:28:02,540
and not one of Aegisthus' men:
they were killed in the halls.'

396
00:28:02,540 --> 00:28:08,040
So he spoke, and my heart broke within me,
and I wept, sitting there in the sand; my heart

397
00:28:08,040 --> 00:28:11,120
had no more will to live and see the light of the sun.

398
00:28:11,120 --> 00:28:16,800
But when I had had my fill of weeping and writhing,
then the unerring Old Man of the Sea said to me:

399
00:28:16,800 --> 00:28:23,840
'No more now, son of Atreus, this long unbending weeping:
we shall find no cure in it. Quick as you can,

400
00:28:23,840 --> 00:28:26,440
try how you may come back to your own fatherland.

401
00:28:26,440 --> 00:28:28,880
Either you will find him alive — or Orestes

402
00:28:28,880 --> 00:28:32,880
has already killed him first,
and you may come in time for the burying.'

403
00:28:32,880 --> 00:28:38,260
So he spoke; and the heart and the proud spirit in
my breast, for all my grieving, were warmed again,

404
00:28:38,260 --> 00:28:41,360
and lifting my voice I spoke winged words to him:

405
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:44,480
'These men I now know of. But name the third man:

406
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:50,180
the one still living, held back on the wide sea —
or dead. Grieved as I am, I want to hear it.'

407
00:28:50,180 --> 00:28:52,600
So I spoke, and he answered me at once:

408
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:55,240
'The son of Laertes, whose home is in Ithaca.

409
00:28:55,240 --> 00:29:00,760
I saw him on an island, letting the big tears fall,
in the halls of the nymph Calypso, who holds him there

410
00:29:00,760 --> 00:29:07,800
by force; and he cannot come to his own fatherland,
for he has no ships at hand, no oars, no companions

411
00:29:07,800 --> 00:29:10,920
to carry him over the broad back of the sea.

412
00:29:10,920 --> 00:29:17,800
But for you, Zeus-nurtured Menelaus, it is not fated
to die in horse-grazing Argos and meet your doom:

413
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:20,160
the deathless ones will carry you to the Elysian plain

414
00:29:20,160 --> 00:29:26,160
and the ends of the earth — where fair-haired
Rhadamanthus is, where life is easiest of all for men:

415
00:29:26,160 --> 00:29:32,440
no snow, no heavy winter, never rain, but always
Ocean sends up the breath of the singing West Wind

416
00:29:32,440 --> 00:29:37,760
to bring men coolness — because Helen is
yours, and you are son-in-law to Zeus.'

417
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:40,460
So speaking he plunged under the surging sea.

418
00:29:40,460 --> 00:29:46,540
And I went to the ships with my godlike companions,
and my heart ran dark with many things as I went.

419
00:29:46,540 --> 00:29:49,600
We made our supper, and ambrosial night came on,

420
00:29:49,600 --> 00:29:52,760
and we lay down to sleep on the surf-line of the sea.

421
00:29:52,760 --> 00:29:55,840
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

422
00:29:55,840 --> 00:29:59,040
first of all we hauled our ships
down to the bright salt water

423
00:29:59,040 --> 00:30:01,740
and set the masts and the sails in the trim ships,

424
00:30:01,740 --> 00:30:05,300
and the men came aboard and took
their places at the oarlocks,

425
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:08,880
and sitting in order they struck
the gray salt water with their oars.

426
00:30:08,880 --> 00:30:14,480
Back to Egypt I brought the ships, to the river
fallen from Zeus, and I offered the full hecatombs;

427
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:17,140
and when I had put to rest the
anger of the gods who are forever,

428
00:30:17,140 --> 00:30:21,880
I heaped a mound for Agamemnon,
that his fame might never be quenched.

429
00:30:21,880 --> 00:30:24,660
That done, I sailed for home, and the immortals gave me

430
00:30:24,660 --> 00:30:27,860
a wind, and brought me quickly to my own dear country.

431
00:30:27,860 --> 00:30:32,040
But come now — stay on in my halls
until the eleventh day, or the twelfth;

432
00:30:32,040 --> 00:30:36,120
then I will send you off with honor,
and give you shining gifts:

433
00:30:36,120 --> 00:30:42,020
three horses and a polished chariot; and besides I
will give you a beautiful cup, so that you may pour

434
00:30:42,020 --> 00:30:45,640
to the deathless gods and remember me all your days."

435
00:30:45,640 --> 00:30:48,660
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

436
00:30:48,660 --> 00:30:51,680
"Son of Atreus, do not hold me here for long.

437
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:56,240
A year I could gladly sit here at your side
and no ache for home or parents would take me:

438
00:30:56,240 --> 00:31:02,680
such strange delight I have in hearing your tales
and your words. But my companions chafe already

439
00:31:02,680 --> 00:31:06,520
in holy Pylos, and you would hold me long here.

440
00:31:06,520 --> 00:31:09,940
As for the gift you would give me —
let it be some treasure.

441
00:31:09,940 --> 00:31:13,040
The horses I will not take to
Ithaca: I will leave them here

442
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:20,920
for you, for your own glory. For you are lord of a
wide plain, where lotus grows in plenty, and galingale,

443
00:31:20,920 --> 00:31:22,900
and wheat and spelt and broad white barley.

444
00:31:22,900 --> 00:31:27,460
In Ithaca there are no wide courses, no meadowland at all:

445
00:31:27,460 --> 00:31:31,420
goat-pasture — and lovelier to me than horse-pasture.

446
00:31:31,420 --> 00:31:34,800
Not one of the islands that lean on
the sea is fit for driving horses,

447
00:31:34,800 --> 00:31:38,440
or rich in meadows — and Ithaca beyond them all."

448
00:31:38,440 --> 00:31:42,240
So he spoke; and Menelaus of the great war-cry smiled,

449
00:31:42,240 --> 00:31:45,800
and stroked him with his hand,
and spoke a word, and named him:

450
00:31:45,800 --> 00:31:49,260
"You are of good blood, dear child — such things you say!

451
00:31:49,260 --> 00:31:52,320
Then I will change the gifts for you; well I can.

452
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:58,220
Of all the treasures lying stored in my house I
will give you the most beautiful, the most honored:

453
00:31:58,220 --> 00:32:03,260
I will give you a wrought mixing-bowl —
all silver it is, and its rims are finished off in gold —

454
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:09,620
the work of Hephaestus. The hero Phaedimus gave it,
king of the Sidonians, when his house sheltered me

455
00:32:09,620 --> 00:32:13,460
on my way home there. This I would send with you."

456
00:32:13,460 --> 00:32:19,500
So the two of them spoke such things to one another,
while the feasters came up to the godlike king's house,

457
00:32:19,500 --> 00:32:23,800
driving sheep before them,
carrying wine that puts heart in men,

458
00:32:23,800 --> 00:32:26,680
and their fair-veiled wives sent bread along with them.

459
00:32:26,680 --> 00:32:32,340
But the suitors, in front of Odysseus' hall,
were amusing themselves with discus and javelin throws

460
00:32:32,340 --> 00:32:37,020
on the leveled ground, insolent as ever,
where they always played.

461
00:32:37,020 --> 00:32:43,440
There Antinous sat, and godlike Eurymachus,
chiefs of the suitors, in prowess far the best of them all.

462
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:49,080
To them Noemon, Phronius' son,
came up and questioned Antinous, and said:

463
00:32:49,080 --> 00:32:53,300
"Antinous, do we know at all — or not —
when Telemachus comes back from sandy Pylos?

464
00:32:53,300 --> 00:32:55,560
He went off with my ship, and now I need her

465
00:32:55,560 --> 00:32:59,140
to cross to Elis of the wide
dancing-grounds, where I keep twelve

466
00:32:59,140 --> 00:33:05,220
brood mares, with hard-working mules at the teat,
unbroken: I would drive one off and break him in."

467
00:33:05,220 --> 00:33:08,720
So he spoke, and their hearts were
astonished: they had never thought

468
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:11,940
he had gone to Neleian Pylos —
surely he was somewhere near,

469
00:33:11,940 --> 00:33:15,480
out at the farms with the flocks, or with the swineherd.

470
00:33:15,480 --> 00:33:18,320
Then Antinous, Eupeithes' son, answered him:

471
00:33:18,320 --> 00:33:21,080
"Tell me the truth of it:
when did he go, and what young men

472
00:33:21,080 --> 00:33:27,460
went with him? Picked men of Ithaca — or his own
hired hands and slaves? He could manage even that!

473
00:33:27,460 --> 00:33:30,780
And tell me this too, honestly, so that I may know it:

474
00:33:30,780 --> 00:33:34,260
did he take the black ship from
you by force, against your will,

475
00:33:34,260 --> 00:33:37,900
or did you give it freely,
because he begged you with fine words?"

476
00:33:37,900 --> 00:33:40,480
Then Noemon, Phronius' son, answered him:

477
00:33:40,480 --> 00:33:45,260
"I gave it freely, myself. What would any man do
when such a man, with cares heavy on his heart,

478
00:33:45,260 --> 00:33:48,360
asked? It would be hard to refuse the gift.

479
00:33:48,360 --> 00:33:52,920
The young men who went with him are the best in the
land after ourselves. And for their leader I marked

480
00:33:52,920 --> 00:33:57,100
Mentor going aboard — or a god, the very image of him.

481
00:33:57,100 --> 00:33:59,700
But here is what I wonder at: I saw noble Mentor

482
00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:04,360
yesterday at dawn, right here —
and he had boarded the ship for Pylos."

483
00:34:04,360 --> 00:34:07,080
So he spoke, and went away to his father's house.

484
00:34:07,080 --> 00:34:09,580
And the proud hearts of the two of them were stunned.

485
00:34:09,580 --> 00:34:15,460
They sat the suitors down in a body and stopped their
games, and among them spoke Antinous, Eupeithes' son,

486
00:34:15,460 --> 00:34:21,640
in anger: his black-brimming heart was hugely filled
with fury, and his eyes were like blazing fire:

487
00:34:21,640 --> 00:34:24,820
"Damn it all — a great work insolently accomplished,

488
00:34:24,820 --> 00:34:28,180
this voyage of Telemachus!
And we said it would come to nothing.

489
00:34:28,180 --> 00:34:31,780
In the teeth of all of us, a mere boy has simply gone:

490
00:34:31,780 --> 00:34:34,520
hauled down a ship, and picked the best men in the land.

491
00:34:34,520 --> 00:34:37,920
He will begin to be our ruin, and soon. May Zeus

492
00:34:37,920 --> 00:34:41,180
destroy his strength before he
reaches the measure of manhood!

493
00:34:41,180 --> 00:34:46,940
But come, give me a fast ship and twenty companions,
so I may watch for him and ambush him on his way home,

494
00:34:46,940 --> 00:34:49,640
in the strait between Ithaca and rugged Samos —

495
00:34:49,640 --> 00:34:53,520
so this sailing after his father
may cost him grim and dear."

496
00:34:53,520 --> 00:34:56,580
So he spoke, and they all approved it and urged it on.

497
00:34:56,580 --> 00:35:00,120
They rose at once and went into the house of Odysseus.

498
00:35:00,120 --> 00:35:05,740
Nor was Penelope long in ignorance of the schemes
the suitors were brewing deep in their hearts:

499
00:35:05,740 --> 00:35:09,340
for the herald Medon told her,
who had overheard their plans,

500
00:35:09,340 --> 00:35:13,060
standing outside the court while
they wove their plot within.

501
00:35:13,060 --> 00:35:16,120
He went through the house to bring the news to Penelope,

502
00:35:16,120 --> 00:35:19,940
and as he crossed the threshold Penelope said to him:

503
00:35:19,940 --> 00:35:22,640
"Herald, why have the lordly suitors sent you in?

504
00:35:22,640 --> 00:35:28,240
To tell the slave-women of godlike Odysseus to
leave their work and make the feast ready for them?

505
00:35:28,240 --> 00:35:35,260
May they never woo again, never gather anywhere else —
may this be the last supper they ever eat here,

506
00:35:35,260 --> 00:35:38,660
they who swarm in day after day and shear away the living,

507
00:35:38,660 --> 00:35:42,380
the substance of clear-headed
Telemachus! Did you never hear,

508
00:35:42,380 --> 00:35:46,240
in years gone by, when you were
children, from your fathers,

509
00:35:46,240 --> 00:35:50,120
what kind of man Odysseus was
among your parents' generation?

510
00:35:50,120 --> 00:35:56,800
Never a deed out of measure, never an unjust word in
all the land — though that is the way of godlike kings:

511
00:35:56,800 --> 00:36:03,140
one mortal a king will hate, another he may love;
but that man never did outrage to a single soul.

512
00:36:03,140 --> 00:36:06,380
No — your hearts are on show, and your ugly works:

513
00:36:06,380 --> 00:36:10,500
there is no gratitude left,
afterward, for good deeds done."

514
00:36:10,500 --> 00:36:14,180
Then Medon answered her, a man of clear-headed sense:

515
00:36:14,180 --> 00:36:17,040
"Would to god, my queen, that this were the worst of it.

516
00:36:17,040 --> 00:36:19,820
A greater evil by far, and harder to bear,

517
00:36:19,820 --> 00:36:24,300
the suitors are plotting now —
may the son of Cronos never accomplish it:

518
00:36:24,300 --> 00:36:27,300
they are bent on cutting Telemachus
down with the sharp bronze

519
00:36:27,300 --> 00:36:33,580
on his way home. He went for news of his father
to holy Pylos and to shining Lacedaemon."

520
00:36:33,580 --> 00:36:38,100
So he spoke, and there where she stood
her knees went loose, and her heart;

521
00:36:38,100 --> 00:36:41,540
for a long time speechlessness held her words; her eyes

522
00:36:41,540 --> 00:36:45,680
filled up with tears; the flower of her voice was stopped.

523
00:36:45,680 --> 00:36:48,840
At long last she found words to answer him:

524
00:36:48,840 --> 00:36:54,180
"Herald, why is my child gone? There was no need to
board the fast-running ships, that are men's horses

525
00:36:54,180 --> 00:36:57,640
of the salt water, and cross the great wet waste.

526
00:36:57,640 --> 00:37:01,500
Is it so that not even his name should be left among men?"

527
00:37:01,500 --> 00:37:04,760
Then Medon answered her, a man of clear-headed sense:

528
00:37:04,760 --> 00:37:10,080
"I do not know whether some god stirred him,
or whether his own heart drove him to go to Pylos, to learn

529
00:37:10,080 --> 00:37:13,380
either his father's homecoming, or what doom he met."

530
00:37:13,380 --> 00:37:16,820
So he spoke, and went away down
through the house of Odysseus.

531
00:37:16,820 --> 00:37:20,160
And heart-eating anguish poured
around her; she could no longer

532
00:37:20,160 --> 00:37:23,180
bear to sit on a chair, though the house was full of them,

533
00:37:23,180 --> 00:37:26,580
but sank down on the threshold
of her richly-worked chamber,

534
00:37:26,580 --> 00:37:33,440
moaning pitiably; and around her whimpered the slave-women,
all of them, young and old, that the house held.

535
00:37:33,440 --> 00:37:36,280
And sobbing hard, Penelope said among them:

536
00:37:36,280 --> 00:37:39,380
"Hear me, dear women. The Olympian has given me sorrows

537
00:37:39,380 --> 00:37:42,680
beyond all the women who were bred and born beside me.

538
00:37:42,680 --> 00:37:48,600
Long ago I lost my noble husband, the lion-hearted,
graced among the Danaans with every excellence,

539
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:53,800
that noble man, whose fame goes wide
through Hellas and the heart of Argos.

540
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:56,940
And now again the storm-winds
have snatched my beloved son,

541
00:37:56,940 --> 00:38:01,340
without glory, out of these halls,
and I never heard him go.

542
00:38:01,340 --> 00:38:06,860
Hard-hearted! Not even you — not one of you —
thought to wake me from my bed, though your hearts knew well

543
00:38:06,860 --> 00:38:09,940
the hour he went down to the black hollow ship.

544
00:38:09,940 --> 00:38:12,620
If I had learned that he was pondering this journey,

545
00:38:12,620 --> 00:38:15,500
then either he would have stayed,
for all his hunger for the road,

546
00:38:15,500 --> 00:38:18,880
or he would have left me dead in these halls behind him.

547
00:38:18,880 --> 00:38:24,560
But now let someone run and call old Dolius, my slave,
whom my father gave me when I first came here,

548
00:38:24,560 --> 00:38:31,000
who keeps my orchard of many trees — so that quickly
he may go sit beside Laertes and tell him all of it.

549
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:36,200
Perhaps Laertes may weave some plan in his heart,
and come out and weep before the people, who are bent

550
00:38:36,200 --> 00:38:40,040
on destroying his line and the line of godlike Odysseus."

551
00:38:40,040 --> 00:38:44,380
Then the dear nurse Eurycleia said to her:

552
00:38:44,380 --> 00:38:47,080
"Dear bride — kill me with the pitiless bronze

553
00:38:47,080 --> 00:38:51,500
or leave me in the house,
but I will not hide the story from you.

554
00:38:51,500 --> 00:38:57,880
I knew all of it. I gave him what he ordered,
bread and sweet wine. And he took a great oath from me

555
00:38:57,880 --> 00:39:03,460
not to tell you before the twelfth day came,
or you missed him yourself and heard that he was gone —

556
00:39:03,460 --> 00:39:06,620
so you would not mar your lovely skin with weeping.

557
00:39:06,620 --> 00:39:12,200
Now bathe, and take clean clothing for your body,
and go up to your upper room with your attendant women,

558
00:39:12,200 --> 00:39:15,100
and pray to Athena, daughter of aegis-bearing Zeus:

559
00:39:15,100 --> 00:39:18,300
she may yet save him, even out of death itself.

560
00:39:18,300 --> 00:39:23,900
And do not grieve a grieving old man more.
I cannot think the blessed gods have wholly hated the line

561
00:39:23,900 --> 00:39:30,340
of Arceisius' son: someone will yet be left to hold
the high-roofed house and the fat fields far around."

562
00:39:30,340 --> 00:39:34,280
So she spoke, and lulled her lament,
and stayed her eyes from weeping.

563
00:39:34,280 --> 00:39:37,400
And she bathed, and took clean clothing for her body,

564
00:39:37,400 --> 00:39:39,880
and went up to her upper room with her attendant women,

565
00:39:39,880 --> 00:39:43,440
and set the barley-grains in a
basket, and prayed to Athena:

566
00:39:43,440 --> 00:39:47,160
"Hear me, child of aegis-bearing Zeus, Unwearied One.

567
00:39:47,160 --> 00:39:53,160
If ever Odysseus of the many designs, in these halls,
burned fat thigh-cuts of ox or sheep for you,

568
00:39:53,160 --> 00:39:59,140
remember them for me now: save my dear son,
and beat away the suitors in their evil overbearing pride."

569
00:39:59,140 --> 00:40:02,560
So she cried the ritual cry;
and the goddess heard her prayer.

570
00:40:02,560 --> 00:40:06,360
But the suitors broke into
uproar through the shadowy halls,

571
00:40:06,360 --> 00:40:08,820
and thus would one of the arrogant young men say:

572
00:40:08,820 --> 00:40:11,300
"So the much-courted queen is making ready a marriage

573
00:40:11,300 --> 00:40:15,080
for one of us — and knows nothing
of the death prepared for her son."

574
00:40:15,080 --> 00:40:17,940
So they would say — knowing nothing of what was prepared.

575
00:40:17,940 --> 00:40:20,400
And Antinous addressed them all, and said:

576
00:40:20,400 --> 00:40:25,720
"Fools! Keep clear of overweening talk of any
kind — or someone may carry the word inside.

577
00:40:25,720 --> 00:40:30,160
Up now, in silence, and let us accomplish
the plan that pleased us all in our hearts."

578
00:40:30,160 --> 00:40:33,000
So he spoke, and picked the twenty best men,

579
00:40:33,000 --> 00:40:36,280
and they went down to the fast
ship and the sand-beach of the sea.

580
00:40:36,280 --> 00:40:41,700
First of all they hauled the ship down to deep water,
and set the mast and the sails in the black ship,

581
00:40:41,700 --> 00:40:46,740
and fitted the oars into their leather slings,
all in due order, and spread the white sails open;

582
00:40:46,740 --> 00:40:49,660
and high-spirited henchmen carried down their arms.

583
00:40:49,660 --> 00:40:52,760
They moored her well out in the water, and came ashore,

584
00:40:52,760 --> 00:40:55,420
and there they took their supper,
and waited for evening to come.

585
00:40:55,420 --> 00:41:02,200
But she in her upper room, watchful-minded Penelope,
lay fasting, tasting neither food nor drink,

586
00:41:02,200 --> 00:41:07,960
turning it over: whether her blameless son would escape
death, or be broken under the overweening suitors.

587
00:41:07,960 --> 00:41:13,880
As a lion turns and turns amid a crowd of men,
in dread, as they draw the treacherous circle round him,

588
00:41:13,880 --> 00:41:16,700
so she was turning when the deep sleep came upon her;

589
00:41:16,700 --> 00:41:20,240
and she sank back and slept,
and all her joints went loose.

590
00:41:20,240 --> 00:41:25,080
Then the goddess thought of one
thing more, gray-eyed Athena.

591
00:41:25,080 --> 00:41:31,600
She made a phantom, fashioned in the likeness of a woman —
Iphthime, daughter of great-hearted Icarius,

592
00:41:31,600 --> 00:41:37,480
wed to Eumelus, whose home was in Pherae —
and sent it to the house of godlike Odysseus,

593
00:41:37,480 --> 00:41:43,540
so that it might bring Penelope, weeping and mourning,
some pause from lamentation and tear-filled grief.

594
00:41:43,540 --> 00:41:49,940
Into the chamber it came, past the strap of the bolt,
and stood above her head, and said to her:

595
00:41:49,940 --> 00:41:53,040
"Do you sleep, Penelope, your heart worn with grief?

596
00:41:53,040 --> 00:41:58,300
No — the gods who live at ease will not have you weep
and be anguished: for your son has his homecoming

597
00:41:58,300 --> 00:42:02,140
still; he is no sinner in the sight of the gods."

598
00:42:02,140 --> 00:42:07,100
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered her —
slumbering very sweetly at the gates of dream:

599
00:42:07,100 --> 00:42:12,480
"Why have you come here, sister? You have not
been used to come before: your home is far away.

600
00:42:12,480 --> 00:42:17,820
And you bid me leave off my misery and all
the pains that crowd my mind and heart.

601
00:42:17,820 --> 00:42:23,480
Long ago I lost my noble husband, the lion-hearted,
graced among the Danaans with every excellence,

602
00:42:23,480 --> 00:42:28,020
that noble man, whose fame goes wide
through Hellas and the heart of Argos.

603
00:42:28,020 --> 00:42:31,280
And now again my beloved son has gone in a hollow ship,

604
00:42:31,280 --> 00:42:34,780
a mere boy, unpracticed in
hardships and the meetings of men.

605
00:42:34,780 --> 00:42:37,580
It is for him I mourn even more than for that other;

606
00:42:37,580 --> 00:42:41,620
for him I tremble, for him I fear,
that something may strike him,

607
00:42:41,620 --> 00:42:44,960
whether among the men of the land
he has gone to, or on the sea.

608
00:42:44,960 --> 00:42:50,460
For many enemies are scheming against him, hungry to
kill him before he comes back to his own country."

609
00:42:50,460 --> 00:42:52,440
And the dim phantom answered her:

610
00:42:52,440 --> 00:42:55,600
"Take heart. Do not fear too greatly in your mind.

611
00:42:55,600 --> 00:43:01,240
Such an escort goes with him as other men have prayed
to have stand beside them — for she has power —

612
00:43:01,240 --> 00:43:04,520
Pallas Athena. And she pities you your grieving:

613
00:43:04,520 --> 00:43:06,660
it is she who sent me now to tell you this."

614
00:43:06,660 --> 00:43:10,040
Then watchful-minded Penelope said to her:

615
00:43:10,040 --> 00:43:13,220
"If you are truly a god,
and have heard the voice of a god,

616
00:43:13,220 --> 00:43:16,840
then come — tell me also of
that other, that man of sorrow:

617
00:43:16,840 --> 00:43:19,900
does he live still somewhere,
and see the light of the sun,

618
00:43:19,900 --> 00:43:23,100
or is he dead already, down in the house of Hades?"

619
00:43:23,100 --> 00:43:25,000
And the dim phantom answered her:

620
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:27,180
"Of him I will not tell the story through —

621
00:43:27,180 --> 00:43:31,780
whether he lives or is dead.
It is evil to speak words empty as wind."

622
00:43:31,780 --> 00:43:37,360
So speaking it slipped away by the doorpost bolt into
the breath of the winds. And the daughter of Icarius

623
00:43:37,360 --> 00:43:40,420
started up out of sleep, and her heart was warmed:

624
00:43:40,420 --> 00:43:44,000
so vivid a dream had rushed upon
her in the dark hour of the night.

625
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:47,440
But the suitors had embarked,
and were sailing the watery ways,

626
00:43:47,440 --> 00:43:50,460
turning over in their hearts sheer murder for Telemachus.

627
00:43:50,460 --> 00:43:55,860
There is a rocky island in the middle of the sea,
midway between Ithaca and rugged Samos —

628
00:43:55,860 --> 00:43:59,220
Asteris, no great place; but there are harbors in it

629
00:43:59,220 --> 00:44:02,234
fit for ships to lurk in, open at either end.
There the Achaeans waited in ambush.
